WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.250 --> 00:00:05.000
So last time we saw Sandor Clegane on Game
of Thrones, the TV adaptation of George R.

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:09.450
R. Martin's Song of Ice and Fire, he is left
for dead by Arya Stark with wounds sustained

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:13.009
in his brutal and really quite gratuitous
fight with Brienne of Tarth

00:00:13.009 --> 00:00:16.850
– a fight which, in the books, never
happened – the injuries that put down the

00:00:16.850 --> 00:00:19.420
Hound were actually from the fight at the Crossroads Inn

00:00:19.420 --> 00:00:23.050
but in any case we are led to
believe that Sandor Clegane is dead.

00:00:23.050 --> 00:00:28.080
However we don't actually see
him die. There's a theory, known as the Gravedigger

00:00:28.080 --> 00:00:32.750
theory, that the Hound not only lives, but
appears in a Brienne chapter of A Feast For

00:00:32.750 --> 00:00:36.960
Crows – after his supposed death in A Storm
Of Swords.

00:00:36.960 --> 00:00:42.329
So what actually happens in that chapter, and why does
it suggest that Sandor Clegane is alive? In

00:00:42.329 --> 00:00:46.039
this chapter, Brienne and companions are
trying to find the Hound in the belief that

00:00:46.039 --> 00:00:51.270
he has Sansa Stark – he actually has – or had
– Arya – but either way Brienne swore an oath

00:00:51.270 --> 00:00:55.090
to find the both of them for their mother
Catelyn. The point is that Brienne and co.,

00:00:55.090 --> 00:01:00.969
while searching for the Hound, pass through a small island in the Riverlands
called the Quiet Isle, which is inhabited

00:01:00.969 --> 00:01:05.400
by brothers of the Faith of the Seven who
have made vows of silence. So Brienne talks

00:01:05.400 --> 00:01:10.070
with the Elder Brother, the only man on the
island allowed to speak (in, by the way, what

00:01:10.070 --> 00:01:14.189
amounts to a hobbit hole) and the Elder Brother
tells her, to her surprise, that the Hound

00:01:14.189 --> 00:01:19.520
is already dead. He says that he came across
the dying Sandor, nursed him until his death,

00:01:19.520 --> 00:01:23.310
and buried him. He then talks for a bit about
the nature of the Hound, about the Hound's

00:01:23.310 --> 00:01:27.970
horse, who is now in the brothers' stable,
and about himself. Brienne tells the Brother

00:01:27.970 --> 00:01:33.450
her life story, reaffirms her vow to find
Arya and/or Sansa, and the chapter ends.

00:01:33.450 --> 00:01:37.829
How is this interpreted to suggest that the
Hound is still alive? It's in the details.

00:01:37.829 --> 00:01:43.159
So, the Elder Brother does say that "the Hound
is dead". But when telling his own story,

00:01:43.159 --> 00:01:47.149
the Elder Brother also says that he himself
"died in the Battle of the Trident". This

00:01:47.149 --> 00:01:52.299
is obviously not a literal death but a metaphorical
death – the end of one identity and the emergence

00:01:52.299 --> 00:01:56.259
of another, which for the Elder Brother involved
a spiritual awakening which took him to the

00:01:56.259 --> 00:02:00.659
Faith and to the Quiet Isle – what he calls
his "second life". So the fact that the Elder

00:02:00.659 --> 00:02:04.759
Brother talks about life and death in this
metaphorical sense calls into question his

00:02:04.759 --> 00:02:10.599
saying that Sandor Clegane is dead – maybe
it too is a metaphorical death. Well – actually

00:02:10.599 --> 00:02:15.040
the Elder Brother only ever says the Hound
is dead, that "the man you hunt" is dead.

00:02:15.040 --> 00:02:19.640
The most he'll say about Sandor Clegane
is that he's "at rest". So what the Elder Brother

00:02:19.640 --> 00:02:23.650
could be getting at is that
the hateful, violent part of Sandor Clegane,

00:02:23.650 --> 00:02:30.000
called the Hound, is metaphorically dead, leaving Sandor Clegane "at rest", and most importantly for us,

00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:34.610
literally alive.
If he's alive, though, where is he? Well,

00:02:34.610 --> 00:02:38.920
on the way up the Quiet Isle, Brienne sees
a brother digging graves who matches Sandor

00:02:38.920 --> 00:02:44.209
pretty much perfectly. Firstly, he's described
as being "bigger than Brienne". Brienne is

00:02:44.209 --> 00:02:48.540
"huge", "well over six feet tall" according
to George R. R. Martin. The Hound is one of the few people

00:02:48.540 --> 00:02:54.569
in Westeros who is, or may be, bigger. Secondly,
the man is described as being "lame" and "half-crippled",

00:02:54.569 --> 00:03:01.569
Sandor could well be lame given his leg was seriously injured at the Crossroads Inn, as was the rest of him, really. Thirdly, Brienne

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:05.849
sees the gravedigger pausing his work to scratch
a dog's ear. It makes sense that Sandor would

00:03:05.849 --> 00:03:09.470
show affection for the animal that is his
sigil, and it's a nice little symbolic hint

00:03:09.470 --> 00:03:13.830
that the gravedigger has some connection with
hounds. Finally, the gravedigger is said to

00:03:13.830 --> 00:03:16.720
be a novice of the brothers, which he would be if he was

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.409
a recently-joined brother of the Quiet Isle.
One other important detail is that many of

00:03:21.409 --> 00:03:26.110
the brothers at Quiet Isle wear "lengths of
wool [around their] lower halves of their faces"

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:30.019
along with robes, so that "all that [can] be seen
of them are their eyes". What this means is

00:03:30.019 --> 00:03:34.060
that the distinctive features of the Hound,
i.e. the disfiguring burns on his face, wouldn't

00:03:34.060 --> 00:03:41.060
be seen or recognised by Brienne, her companions,
or anyone else. So this gravedigger is suspiciously like Sandor Clegane.

00:03:43.390 --> 00:03:47.670
A final piece of evidence involves the Hound's
horse, named Stranger. The horse is known

00:03:47.670 --> 00:03:52.650
to have an extremely violent temperament,
biting and kicking anyone who isn't Sandor.

00:03:52.650 --> 00:03:56.620
The brothers at Quiet Isle are no exception
– Stranger broke one brother's shin and bit

00:03:56.620 --> 00:04:01.120
an ear off another. So, if we are to believe
that Sandor Clegane really did literally die,

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:06.280
how on Earth – or on whatever the hells kind of planet a Song of Ice and Fire is set on – did the Elder Brother

00:04:06.280 --> 00:04:11.340
get Stranger to the Quiet Isle? What makes
it really implausible is the nature of the

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:15.400
path to Quiet Isle. When Brienne and companions
are taken to the Isle, we see what Septon

00:04:15.400 --> 00:04:20.880
Meribald calls "The path of faith". It's apparently
the only way to the Isle, and it's a maze-like

00:04:20.880 --> 00:04:26.330
unmarked natural path "at least a mile and
a half" long across an expanse of mud where you

00:04:26.330 --> 00:04:30.090
have to be "careful where [you] set [your] feet" or
you'll be swallowed by quicksand, or end up

00:04:30.090 --> 00:04:34.470
drowning when the tide comes in. It's so dangerous
that when Septon Meribald leads Brienne and

00:04:34.470 --> 00:04:38.540
co. across, he has to periodically stop and
poke the ground ahead with his staff to find

00:04:38.540 --> 00:04:44.090
safe passage. Can we really believe that the
Elder Brother, on his own, took such a wild

00:04:44.090 --> 00:04:48.710
and dangerous horse on such a treacherous
path? Why would the Elder Brother even risk

00:04:48.710 --> 00:04:54.300
trying? What makes far more sense is that
while the Hound metaphorically dies, Sandor

00:04:54.300 --> 00:04:59.400
Clegane is literally – well, and metaphorically – healed by the Elder Brother – who, by the

00:04:59.400 --> 00:05:05.009
way, is said to have miraculous healing hands – so the
one man who can control Stranger walks the

00:05:05.009 --> 00:05:09.729
"path of faith" with the Elder Brother in
relative safety, and becomes a novice of the

00:05:09.729 --> 00:05:13.729
brothers. It seems likely that the Elder Brother
becomes a friend or mentor to Sandor, as this

00:05:13.729 --> 00:05:17.050
would explain the deep knowledge the brother
has of his nature

00:05:17.050 --> 00:05:21.770
So there you go. There is pretty compelling
textual evidence, or strong hints, at least,

00:05:21.770 --> 00:05:27.039
that Sandor Clegane is still alive, living
as a monk of sorts on Quiet Isle and possibly

00:05:27.039 --> 00:05:31.090
atoning for a life of violence, just as the
Elder Brother is atoning for his career as

00:05:31.090 --> 00:05:38.090
a soldier. It gives a really satisfying, if
entirely implicit, conclusion to his character

00:05:38.120 --> 00:05:43.630
arc. Because Sandor is a significant and complex
character. Unlike his brother, who seems a

00:05:43.630 --> 00:05:47.660
simple beast to the core, Sandor has some
sensitivity, some humanity,

00:05:47.660 --> 00:05:52.409
which he hides behind a quite literal
mask of thoughtless animalistic aggression

00:05:52.409 --> 00:05:59.409
– the Hound. It's not entirely unlike Tyrion
and dwarfism – "never forget what you are ... wear it like armor" –

00:05:59.780 --> 00:06:04.180
but it's rather more like Slim Shady and Marshall
Mathers, if I can use that analogy with a

00:06:04.180 --> 00:06:09.240
straight face. Aggression to hide emotional
insecurity, y'know? Anyway

00:06:09.240 --> 00:06:13.220
so Sandor has two identities, burned and unburned, hound

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:15.639
and man, brutal and kind, fearless

00:06:15.639 --> 00:06:19.289
and fearful,
fire and ice, and this conflict hurts him,

00:06:19.289 --> 00:06:23.039
driving him to drinking and disregard for
his own life.

00:06:23.039 --> 00:06:28.030
Arya leaves the Hound for dead, and in a way,
the Hound does die. The Elder Brother helps

00:06:28.030 --> 00:06:33.350
Sandor exorcise his darker half, remove the
Hound helmet for good, and find peace, and

00:06:33.350 --> 00:06:37.090
"rest", at the Quiet Isle. This is
why the Elder Brother lies to Brienne, or

00:06:37.090 --> 00:06:40.400
at least chooses to be a tad indirect with
the truth.

00:06:40.400 --> 00:06:41.660
It's understandable that the Elder

00:06:41.660 --> 00:06:44.039
Brother would want to protect his new novice, this

00:06:44.039 --> 00:06:47.979
man who he has worked to enlighten, and with
whom he probably identifies with given their

00:06:47.979 --> 00:06:53.349
similar pasts.
It's funny, in a way. The "worst shit in the

00:06:53.349 --> 00:06:57.680
Seven Kingdoms" is one of the only characters
in the Song of Ice and Fire to get a happy

00:06:57.680 --> 00:07:01.740
ending, while the honourable men and the heroic
men are are killed and discredited. There's

00:07:01.740 --> 00:07:06.370
no justice in the "game of thrones" – wrongdoers
not only get away with their crimes, but often

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:10.789
end up better off than the good guys. The
gravedigger theory can be seen as consistent

00:07:10.789 --> 00:07:14.360
with that theme.
One last thing – and this is where show watchers

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:19.440
should stop watching this video spoiler warning,
spoiler warning, spoiler warning – it's kind

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.849
of obligatory at this point to recognise that
many fans of A Song of Ice and Fire not only believe that

00:07:23.849 --> 00:07:28.539
Sandor is alive on Quiet Isle but expect him
to return to King's Landing to confront his

00:07:28.539 --> 00:07:34.930
now undead and possibly headless brother Robert
Strong in Cersei's trial by combat. This theory,

00:07:34.930 --> 00:07:41.210
or rather this wild, fanboyish speculation,
is known as Cleganebowl. Oh, excuse me, er, it's

00:07:41.210 --> 00:07:43.000
known as CLEGANEBOWL. 

00:07:43.000 --> 00:07:55.000
&lt;mad beats&gt;

00:07:55.000 --> 00:07:57.400
The evidence for cleganebowl doesn't extend far beyond

00:07:57.430 --> 00:08:01.979
'it would be like, totally awesome man'. While
it's true that Sandor Clegane has wanted to

00:08:01.979 --> 00:08:05.819
kill his brother for some time, what we've
learned at Quiet Isle strongly suggests he's

00:08:05.819 --> 00:08:11.840
gotten over that hatred. Sandor has put down
his helm, put down his sword, put down his anger and found peace

00:08:11.840 --> 00:08:17.919
as a monk, a crippled monk on a secluded
island far from King's Landing. How and why would

00:08:17.919 --> 00:08:23.020
he want to fight in the trial? Why would anyone
let him be the champion of the faith, monk

00:08:23.020 --> 00:08:27.319
or not? Why would he not be executed the day
he walks into King's Landing given that the last

00:08:27.319 --> 00:08:30.900
time he was there he abandoned the king he was
sworn to protect? "Fuck the king". There's

00:08:30.900 --> 00:08:36.240
just no compelling evidence, so Cleganebowl
has to be considered a distant possibility at best,

00:08:36.240 --> 00:08:42.909
albeit a totally awesome one.
So yeah. That's Cleganebowl, and that's the Gravedigger

00:08:42.909 --> 00:08:48.470
theory. I hope you enjoyed this video. If
you're interested, a video explaining the

00:08:48.470 --> 00:08:53.570
deal with Robert Strong is a possibility,
among others. So let me know. Either way, you

00:08:53.570 --> 00:08:58.800
can definitely expect more A Song of Ice and Fire fan theory
videos fairly soon. If you can't wait, check

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.670
out these sites for what's probably the best
A Song of Ice and Fire discussion and analysis on the Internet.

00:09:03.670 --> 00:09:07.370
I'd like to make very clear that I don't claim
credit for coming up with these theories.

00:09:07.370 --> 00:09:11.940
I may add my own ideas, but they are ultimately
a collective effort, notably by the users

00:09:11.940 --> 00:09:14.510
of these sites.
So there are now Facebook

00:09:14.510 --> 00:09:18.890
and Twitter pages for this channel if you're into that sort
of thing, where you can be notified of new

00:09:18.890 --> 00:09:23.730
videos or whatever else is going on. Finally,
I'd like to sincerely thank everyone who has

00:09:23.730 --> 00:09:27.670
watched and subscribed and shared and left
